    "narrative": "The slate for Ghost Ride the WHIP centers on the high-upside matchup for **Corbin Carroll** (79.66) against Quintana. While **Corbin Carroll** faces a southpaw, his elite power-speed profile remains a must-start, bolstered by an excellent 5.24 xERA opposing starter and a +19% boost from SP skill metrics. Similarly, **Josh Naylor** (72.02) provides our anchor at 1B, capitalizing on a +10% SP skill advantage against Lugo, who profile as a targetable arm for our lineup today. **James Wood** (69.92) also stands out as a premier play; batting leadoff against P\u00e9rez, his combination of favorable order placement and a solid 0.379 xwOBA makes him an essential component of our daily efficiency goals.\n\nThe most difficult decision on the bench involved **Carson Williams** (46.91), who fell just shy of the active roster due to a suppressed score resulting from a #9 hole in the lineup. Despite his potential for stolen bases, the optimizer favored the higher projected floor of **Jose Caballero** (49.54), whose +2% adjustment for slow delivery against the opposing pitcher provided just enough variance to keep him in the OF3 slot. We are keeping a close watch on **Brice Turang** (49.06); while his speed is a critical asset, the -17% SP skill penalty against Yamamoto highlights a significant risk, and we will monitor his early at-bats closely to see if that elite hold pitcher limits our stolen base opportunities as projected. \n\nOverall, our strategy relies on the favorable platoon splits of **Vinnie Pasquantino** (+14% platoon edge) and **Travis Bazzana** (+7% platoon edge, +10% order bonus). While we are dealing with the absence of **Trevor Story** and **Mickey Moniak** on the IL, our depth remains intact. We are operating with high caution regarding **Dalton Rushing** (51.29), as his #7 spot in the order and the opposing catcher\u2019s elite hold rating (33% SB prevention) force us to lower expectations for his stolen base contributions today.",
